From 216c38075d44c40a697b0b3fcc21eb999ae0bf57 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SteveDeFacto <5148995+SteveDeFacto@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 11:09:27 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Legacy CPU image monitor: the partition's processor cap
 binds, not the catalog cpuPercent

The installed legacy CPU image (runtime.bin 5fbfb947, monitor from this tree) put every bundle domain in cgroup
cpu.max = catalog cpuPercent (EffectivePolicy: the bundle wins; a load's cpu was silently ignored), so an app whose
purchased share gives its partition several VPs could still use one CPU. allocatedPolicy treats CPU like the existing
RAM allocation: an explicit load cpu RAISES the quota when catalog <= cpu <= online CPUs*100 and is refused otherwise
(no lowering, no unbounded override, never ignored). On an image whose measured platform directory carries
cpu-vm-bound.enabled (one app per Hyper-V partition with an exact processor Limit/Reservation), a domain loaded with no
cpu gets every online processor, so the partition's cap - set and read back by the manager - is what binds; that works
with the installed loader, which sends no cpu. Without the marker the catalog share is kept exactly as before. The
manifest, AppID and every report still name the catalog policy; the load answer states the applied cpuPercent.
Tests: allocatedPolicy cases, the cgroup writer, and the real load path through the startDomain seam (on the seam-only
commit the same load test shows cpu.max "100000 100000" where 336 or all online processors were due).
